The **Beginnings Society** is an unregistered and unofficial society said to operate on the campus of Stanford University. It is known mainly through anonymous online posts attributed to **Wenitte Apiou**, who is regarded by followers as its founder and by critics as its self-appointed prophet. The group is reported to hold small, invitation-only gatherings at which members take psilocybin. Stanford has not confirmed that the group exists.<sup>[citation needed]</sup>

## History

### Precedents

The Society's supporters frame it as part of a long lineage of psychedelic activity on American campuses, and two precedents come up most often.

**Harvard (1960s).** In 1960, Timothy Leary and Richard Alpert began the Harvard Psilocybin Project. The 1962 Good Friday Experiment at Marsh Chapel, run by Walter Pahnke, gave psilocybin to divinity students to study mystical experience. By 1963 the project had collapsed amid controversy. Alpert was dismissed and Leary's time at Harvard ended soon after. Later psychedelic work in the circle expanded to LSD. Wenitte's posts treat this as a warning about what happens when a campus conversation becomes public and personality-driven.

**Stanford (1950s to 1960s).** In 1959, writer Ken Kesey took part in government-funded LSD experiments at the Menlo Park VA hospital, a short drive from campus. Menlo Park was also home to early psychedelic therapy research. Stanford today hosts mainstream clinical research on psilocybin, ketamine, and MDMA, a fact members tend to ignore because it undermines the mystique.

### Founding (2026)

According to the one primary source, a post by Apiou on r/Stanford, the Society's first gathering took place in a basement room on campus. Eleven participants, previously strangers, reportedly shared psilocybin. The post gives no account of dosing, sourcing, or procedure. It says only that the room fell silent afterward and that one attendee spoke the first words, "I think I've been asleep my whole life."

The group's name is never explained. Apiou has said it has "no name on any registry."

### The FuturPedia repository

The post stated that admission to a second gathering would not be granted on request. Applicants were told to find an apparently abandoned repository on the FuturPedia GitHub and solve a hidden sequence in its commit history. Apiou discouraged requests for hints and comments asking whether the post was real. The repository has no stars and a sparse readme, which observers consider consistent with its purpose.

## Reception

Responses split into three camps. Supporters describe the Society as contemplative and secretive by design. Skeptics call it an elaborate hoax or alternate reality game. A third group, mostly commenters in student forums, call it a cult, a label Apiou has not rejected outright. Apiou has asked followers to stop using a nickname they coined for him.

Whether the Society is real remains disputed. Apiou's stated position is that the question "depends on whether you show up."

